# Summary
Introduced: the newly added ignore-case condition evaluators use ASCII-only case-insensitive comparison for general policy strings, including user-controlled UTF-8 tag values.
The commit adds ignore-case bucket-policy operators but implements all of them with `str::eq_ignore_ascii_case`. That routine only folds ASCII A-Z/a-z. S3 object tag keys and values are parsed as UTF-8 and may contain non-ASCII characters, so a policy author using these operators with non-ASCII labels gets incorrect authorization results. For example, an Allow guarded by `StringNotEqualsIgnoreCase` intended to allow all classifications except `sëcret`, or a Deny guarded by `StringEqualsIgnoreCase` intended to block `sëcret` in any case, can be bypassed by using a differently cased non-ASCII variant such as `SËCRET`, because the evaluator treats `ë` and `Ë` as different.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the commit introduced ignore-case bucket-policy operators and that they compare with ASCII-only `eq_ignore_ascii_case`.
- [x] Confirm user-controlled request object tags can reach those operators for supported S3 actions such as `PutObject`.
- [x] Confirm tag parsing accepts percent-encoded multibyte UTF-8 non-ASCII values without restricting them to ASCII.
- [x] Dynamically reproduce incorrect authorization outcomes for non-ASCII case variants and include an ASCII-only control case.
