## Report
Validated the finding with a targeted Rust PoC against the real `auth` policy evaluator and `server_http::http::xml::parse_url_encoded_tags`. Code review confirmed the introduced ignore-case evaluators use Rust `str::eq_ignore_ascii_case`: `crates/auth/src/bucket_policy/condition_op.rs:312-320` for `StringEqualsIgnoreCase` and `crates/auth/src/bucket_policy/condition_op.rs:418-426` for `StringNotEqualsIgnoreCase`. Request object tags are accepted as evaluable condition input: `crates/auth/src/bucket_policy/condition_key.rs:119-123` registers `s3:RequestObjectTag/` with `AnyEvaluable`, `condition_key.rs:285-287` resolves the request tag value, and `condition_key.rs:597-620` dispatches to the operator. User-supplied URL tags decode UTF-8 without an ASCII-only restriction: `crates/server-http/src/http/xml.rs:3380-3398` parses key/value pairs, `xml.rs:3400-3416` only enforces length/aws-prefix checks, and `xml.rs:3445-3468` percent-decodes bytes then calls `String::from_utf8`. Dynamic PoC output: `parsed tag: [("classification", "SËCRET")]`; `std eq_ignore_ascii_case('sëcret','SËCRET') = false`; `Deny StringEqualsIgnoreCase(sëcret) with actual SËCRET => NoMatch`; `Allow StringNotEqualsIgnoreCase(sëcret) with actual SËCRET => ExplicitAllow`; ASCII-only control `SëCRET` produced `ExplicitDeny`. This demonstrates both a Deny bypass and an unintended Allow for non-ASCII case variants. Per required compiled-stack flow, I attempted a direct assertion-failure reproduction, attempted valgrind (`valgrind_unavailable` in this container), and used LLDB. The LLDB trace stopped inside `auth::bucket_policy::condition_op::eval_string_equals_ignore_case` at `condition_op.rs:316`, with the stack flowing through `condition_key::evaluate_clause` at `condition_key.rs:619`, `condition_clause_matches_request` at `bucket_policy.rs:1445`, `PolicyStatement::condition_match_result` at `bucket_policy.rs:769`, and `BucketPolicy::evaluate` at `bucket_policy.rs:87`, confirming the real vulnerable evaluator path.

