## Narrative
The finding is real and reachable in the main product. argmin-s3 exposes an HTTP S3 API and authenticates requests before dispatch. PutObject, CopyObject, multipart creation, and streaming PutObject parse attacker-controlled x-amz-tagging headers using parse_url_encoded_tags. That parser percent-decodes bytes and accepts any valid UTF-8 with length/count checks, so non-ASCII tag values are valid request inputs. server-core then includes request tags in PolicyRequest when a bucket policy references s3:RequestObjectTag, and the auth operator table marks StringEqualsIgnoreCase/StringNotEqualsIgnoreCase as evaluable. The vulnerable evaluators use Rust eq_ignore_ascii_case, so ë and Ë compare unequal. As validated earlier, this can turn Deny StringEqualsIgnoreCase(sëcret) with actual SËCRET into NoMatch and Allow StringNotEqualsIgnoreCase(sëcret) into ExplicitAllow. Severity remains medium rather than high because exploitation requires a specific bucket-policy configuration using ignore-case operators with non-ASCII authorization labels; it does not bypass SigV4 generally, steal credentials, or provide broad unauthenticated access by itself.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication is enforced before normal S3 dispatch; missing auth is represented as anonymous for public bucket/policy decisions.
- Bucket policy deny/allow evaluation exists and deny normally overrides allow.
- Default listener is localhost 127.0.0.1:9000, but ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR can expose the service on other interfaces.
- TLS is optional via ARGMIN_TLS_CERT_PATH and ARGMIN_TLS_KEY_PATH.
- Tag parsing enforces tag count and key/value length limits and rejects invalid UTF-8, but intentionally permits valid non-ASCII UTF-8.
- Connection and in-flight request limits are configurable, but they do not mitigate this authorization logic issue.
## Blindspots
- Static review did not inspect deployment manifests or network infrastructure; no public ingress or load balancer artifact was present in the checked evidence.
- Exact AWS/IAM Unicode case-folding semantics were not verified from live cloud APIs because cloud API calls are out of scope.
- Impact depends on operator-created bucket policies and data-classification practices, which are deployment-specific and not fully knowable from repository artifacts.
- Review focused on the provided finding path and did not exhaustively enumerate every condition key affected by the shared operator table.
